ow all we have is  y plus 17 equals x plus 3 squared we  want to get Y by itself so we're just  going to subtract 17 from both sides of  the equation and so that gives us y  equals the quantity X plus 3 squared  minus 17 and now you can see that it's  in this form right here  the  vertex is going to be the opposite of  positive 3 that's negative 3 and this is  going to be negative 17 so that's our  vertex the a value is positive once we  know the parabola is going to open up  and you've got it okay number two now  we've got y equals two x squared minus  eight X plus one notice we have that  leading coefficient a which is not equal  to one so this is a little bit more  challenging but the first step is we  want to get the constant this number on  the other side with the Y so just  subtract one we've got Y minus one  equals two times x squared minus 8x now  the next step is I'm going to factor out  this two out of both of these terms so  it's just like doing the distributive  property backwards or factoring out or  dividing out two and what I'd like to do  is I like to leave a little space here  for completing the square so what you  want to do is you want to take this  coefficient the number in front of the  ax you want to divide it by two and  square it so that's negative four  divided by two is negative two negative  two squared is positive four I'm going  to add four here but the here is where  students sometimes go a little bit off  the tracks they say hmm if I edit four  here Mario don't add four over here but  because this is inside of the  parenthesis this four it's actually two  times four so really out of thin air  we're adding eight to the right side of  the equation we have to add eight to the  left side of the equation so that's  going to give us Y plus seven equals two  and then now we're going to factor this  and it's a perfect square  perfect trinomial here and so we're  gonna write it as a binomial squared and  remember it's always half of that middle  coefficient so this is going to be X  minus two the quantity squared if this  was x squared plus four X then it would  be X plus two the quantity squared now  we're almost there we want to get Y by  itself so let's just subtract seven from  both sides of the equation and we get y  equals two times X minus two squared  minus seven and you can see now the  vertex is going to be at positive two  this one's the opposite negative seven  this one's the same a value is positive  two that two is gonna stretch the  parabola making a little bit narrower  and it's going to open up because it's  positive and you've got it Before we  jump into number three if we haven't met  yet in your  to the channel my name is Mario from  Mario's math tutoring and what I do is I  tutor students full-time but what I do  is I take what I learned from working  with my students and I try to condense  it down and take the simplest and the  best way of explaining some of these  problems so that I can share them with  you so you can get the benefit too and  my goal for the channel is to really you  know make learning math less stressful  so you can raise your grade pass your  class and go on to pursue your dreams  and so that's what we're all about here  let's go into the last problem and I  want to really show you how to complete  the square with these more difficult  ones and again that first step is you  want to subtract 3 from both sides of  the equation okay and the reason you're  doing that is you're just trying to get  that number out of the way okay so that  you can complete the square a little bit  more easily so now we're left with  because these cancel is negative 1/2 x  squared minus 4x you don't want that  leading coefficient to be something  other than one it makes it difficult to  complete the square so what I like to do  is I like to factor out the leading  coefficient and when you factor  something out it's really like dividing  it out so you're it's like dividing out  negative one half but what we do is we  put that negative 1/2 in front of the  parenthesis like this this is going to  cancel that's going to give us x squared  remember when you divide by a fraction  it's like multiplying by the reciprocal  so this is really like multiplying by  negative two which is going to give us  positive 8x and then I like to leave a  little space for completing the square  now if you're not sure if you did it  right you can always distribute the  negative 1/2 that's gonna give you a  negative 1/2 x squared negative 1/2  times 8x gives you back the negative 4x  and you can see it matches so you know  you're on the right track and then now  when we complete the square we say  what's half of this middle coefficient  squared so I like to do that work a  little bit off on the side here 8  divided by 2 is 4 and then 4 squared is  16 now if I add 16 to the right you're  probably saying well Mario you have to  add 16 to the left but because it's in  parenthesis it's actually negative 1/2  times 16 that's like negative 8 then  again we're adding out of thin air  negative 8 to the right side we have to  add negative 8 to the left side to keep  the equation balanced we don't want to  change you know the overall equation we  just want to change the way that it  looks we just want to change the form so  now  this is a perfect square we can factor  it and it's going to factor 2x plus 4  the quantity squared remember it's  always half of that middle coefficient  if this was negative 8x I would write  negative for the last step you want to  get Y by itself so I'm just going to add  11 to both sides of the equation and  that gives us y equals negative 1/2 the  quantity X plus 4 squared plus 11 so our  vertex is going to be at negative 4  positive 11 and the a value is negative  which means it opens down the 1/2 is  going to make the graph wider so it's  like a vertical shrink or vertical  compression and you got it the other  thing that you learn about when you're  doing this completing the square is  solving equations by completing the  square and I talked about that in that  video right there so follow me over  there to get some more practice with  completing the square and solving  equations by completing the square  I'll see you in that video

